   2008), Atizapan de Zaragoza, MEXICO, OCT 27-31, 2008},
Organization = {Mexican Soc Artificial Intelligence; Tecnol Monterrey, Campus Estado
   Mexico},
Abstract = {Learning how to count in different bases has been seen as a trivial task
   in almost all introductory 7 mathematics courses. However the low
   performance shown by many students, while performing this task, is
   appalling. This situation has motivated serious research in this matter
   In order to study a model of count learning, we analyze the performance
   of a multilayer perceptron that learns to count in several bases (5, 10,
   13, 20, 60). We give evidence that it is not equivalent for the model to
   learn to count in all base as the errors are not equivalent, biased
   toward a low error when the task is to learn to count in base 20. When
   the task is to learn to count in all bases following a given sequence,
   the model shows non-equivalent errors for some bases. This may sized
   some light on education planning that can result in better introductory
   courses.},
